{
  "step_1": "The decision reason can be applied to the decision problem. The maximum magnitude outcome for Lottery A is 1000 Euros (with 50% probability), and for Lottery B it is 500 Euros (with 100% probability). The more favorable maximum magnitude outcome is 1000 Euros in Lottery A. The reason evaluates this outcome by weighting it with its probability (1000 × 0.5 = 500), whereas for Lottery B, the maximum magnitude outcome is 500 × 1.0 = 500. Although both have the same expected value for the maximum outcome, the reason favors the higher outcome (1000) even if it is probabilistic, as long as the weighted comparison is part of the rule. The rule implies a preference for the lottery with the higher outcome if the difference in probability-weighted magnitude is favorable or if the focus is on the high-end outcome. In this case, Lottery A has the higher maximum magnitude outcome, so the reason would lean toward preferring A, depending on how strictly the weighting is applied.",
